= Behavior =

'''Behavior''' is the activity of an organism, individual, group, or operationally defined system in relation to internal state and external conditions. In biology and psychology, behavior includes actions and coordinated patterns such as movement, communication, feeding, avoidance, exploration, learning, social interaction, and in some definitions deliberate or coordinated inaction.

There is no single definition accepted across all behavioral disciplines. The American Psychological Association uses a broad definition that includes an organism's activities in response to internal or external stimuli, while behavioral biologists have documented substantial disagreement about what exactly counts as behavior.<ref name="apa">[https://dictionary.apa.org/behavior APA Dictionary of Psychology, “behavior.”]</ref><ref name="levitis">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2760923/ Levitis, D. A., Lidicker, W. Z. Jr., and Freund, G. (2009), “Behavioural biologists don't agree on what constitutes behaviour,” ''Animal Behaviour'' 78(1), 103–110.]</ref>

A useful scientific definition should therefore specify the unit of analysis and measurement criteria instead of assuming that every change in a living system is behavior in the same sense.

== Organism and environment ==

Behavior is often studied as a relation between an organism and its environment.

A simple descriptive form is

<pre>
internal state + external conditions
→ coordinated activity
→ environmental and bodily consequences
</pre>

The organism is not a passive input-output device. Internal physiological state, memory, learning history, goals, hormonal state, and development can change how the same external condition influences action.

Likewise, behavior changes the environment and thereby alters future stimuli.

== What counts as behavior? ==

Levitis, Lidicker and Freund surveyed behavioral biologists and found inconsistent intuitions about ambiguous cases. They proposed defining behavior as internally coordinated responses, including actions or inactions, of whole living organisms or groups to internal or external stimuli, while excluding changes more readily treated as development.<ref name="levitis" />

This definition captures several useful boundaries:

* behavior normally concerns the whole organism or coordinated group;
* behavior includes response to internal as well as external conditions;
* inaction can be behavioral when it is coordinated;
* growth or developmental transformation is not automatically behavior.

Other disciplines draw boundaries differently.

== Overt and covert behavior ==

'''Overt behavior''' is directly observable action.

Examples include:

* walking;
* vocalizing;
* pressing a button;
* grooming;
* fleeing.

Some psychological traditions also use '''covert behavior''' for internal activities such as subvocal speech or other processes not directly observable without instruments or self-report.

Whether internal cognition should itself be called behavior is theory-dependent.

BITwiki therefore distinguishes [[Behavior]] from [[Cognition]] while recognizing that some psychological definitions overlap them.

== Behavior and physiology ==

Physiological activity can cause, constrain, or accompany behavior without itself being classified as behavior at the same level.

A change in heart rate may be a physiological response.

Running away is a behavioral action.

The distinction depends on the research question.

Behavioral neuroscience explicitly links neural and physiological mechanisms to observed actions, so the levels should be connected rather than treated as independent.

== Tinbergen's four questions ==

Niko Tinbergen's 1963 framework remains foundational for behavioral biology. He argued that a comprehensive account of behavior asks four complementary questions:<ref name="tinbergen">[https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1439-0310.1963.tb01161.x Tinbergen, N. (1963), “On aims and methods of Ethology,” ''Zeitschrift für Tierpsychologie'' 20, 410–433.]</ref>

'''Mechanism''' — what immediate sensory, neural, hormonal, or environmental processes produce the behavior?

'''Ontogeny''' — how does the behavior develop across the organism's lifetime?

'''Function''' — how does the behavior affect survival or reproduction under relevant ecological conditions?

'''Phylogeny''' — how did the behavior and its underlying mechanisms evolve?

These questions are complementary rather than competing explanations.

== Proximate and evolutionary explanation ==

Mechanism and development are often grouped as '''proximate''' questions.

Function and phylogeny are often grouped as '''evolutionary''' or '''ultimate''' questions.

For example, bird song can be studied in terms of:

* neural circuits and hormones;
* learning during development;
* effects on mate choice or territory;
* evolutionary history across species.

A neural explanation does not replace an evolutionary explanation, and vice versa.

== Innate and learned behavior ==

The distinction between innate and learned behavior is useful but often oversimplified.

Behavior can be influenced by:

* genetic variation;
* developmental conditions;
* learning;
* social experience;
* current environment.

Many behaviors reflect interactions among these factors.

Calling a behavior “innate” should not imply that learning, development, or environment are irrelevant.

Calling a behavior “learned” should not imply the absence of biological constraints.

== Learning ==

[[Learning]] is a relatively persistent change in behavior or behavioral capacity associated with experience.

Major learning processes include:

* habituation;
* sensitization;
* classical conditioning;
* operant conditioning;
* observational learning;
* skill learning.

Behavioral change can also occur through fatigue, maturation, injury, drugs, or temporary motivation.

Not every change in performance is learning.

== Motivation and internal state ==

The same stimulus can produce different behavior depending on internal state.

Foraging can depend on hunger.

Threat responses can depend on stress, reproductive condition, social context, or previous experience.

Models of behavior therefore often include latent state variables rather than assuming a fixed stimulus-response mapping.

== Ethology ==

'''Ethology''' is the biological study of behavior, historically emphasizing observation of animals in natural or ecologically meaningful contexts.

Modern ethology integrates:

* field observation;
* experiments;
* physiology;
* genetics;
* neurobiology;
* evolutionary analysis.

Tinbergen emphasized careful description before explanation.<ref name="tinbergen" />

== Behaviorism ==

'''Behaviorism''' refers to psychological traditions that emphasized observable behavior and environmental contingencies.

Different behaviorist schools made different claims about mental states.

Behavior analysis developed rigorous experimental methods for relations among antecedents, actions, and consequences.

Modern behavioral science is broader and includes cognitive, neural, genetic, and computational approaches.

== Ethograms ==

An '''ethogram''' is an operational catalog of behavioral categories used in animal-behavior research.

A good ethogram defines categories so different observers can apply them consistently.

Example categories might include:

* feeding;
* locomotion;
* grooming;
* aggression;
* resting;
* courtship.

Vague labels such as “anxious” should be avoided unless operational criteria are defined.

== Behavioral measurement ==

Common measures include:

'''Frequency''' — how often an event occurs.

'''Duration''' — how long it lasts.

'''Latency''' — time from a defined event to the behavior.

'''Intensity''' — magnitude according to a defined scale.

'''Sequence''' — order of behavioral events.

'''Probability''' — likelihood under specified conditions.

The chosen metric should match the phenomenon.

== Sampling ==

Continuous observation is not always possible.

Behavioral sampling methods include:

* focal-animal sampling;
* scan sampling;
* event sampling;
* interval recording.

Each creates different biases.

Rare events can be missed by scan sampling.

Event sampling can overrepresent conspicuous behaviors.

The method should be reported explicitly.

== Observer reliability ==

When humans classify behavior, observer agreement should be measured.

Two observers can watch the same event and assign different categories.

Training, blinded coding, explicit definitions, and inter-rater reliability improve reproducibility.

Agreement is not proof that the categories are biologically meaningful.

== Experimental manipulation ==

Causal behavioral research manipulates candidate causes while controlling alternatives.

Examples include:

* changing resource availability;
* presenting sensory stimuli;
* altering social context;
* pharmacological manipulation;
* lesions or neural stimulation;
* genetic intervention.

Ethical constraints vary by species and procedure.

Observational association alone cannot establish mechanism.

== Social behavior ==

Social behavior includes interactions among members of the same or different species.

Examples include:

* cooperation;
* aggression;
* courtship;
* parental care;
* communication;
* dominance;
* collective movement.

Behavior can depend on the actions of others, producing feedback loops and strategic interaction.

== Collective behavior ==

Groups can exhibit organized behavior not easily reducible to one individual's action.

Examples include:

* flocking;
* schooling;
* swarming;
* coordinated group hunting.

These patterns can emerge from local interaction rules.

Group-level description should not imply a group mind unless independent evidence supports such a claim.

== Behavior and cognition ==

[[Cognition]] concerns information processing, representation, memory, decision, and related mental or computational processes.

Behavior is often the observable evidence used to infer cognition.

The inference requires care:

<pre>
same behavior
≠ necessarily same cognitive mechanism
</pre>

Different mechanisms can produce similar actions.

Conversely, similar cognitive states can produce different behavior under different constraints.

== Behavior and sensory processing ==

[[Sensory processing]] transforms physical or chemical input into neural or functional representations that can influence behavior.

Behavioral responses can therefore reveal sensory sensitivity.

But a behavioral threshold can differ from a receptor or neural threshold because motivation and decision criteria intervene.

== Behavior and homeostasis ==

[[Homeostasis]] can generate behavior.

An organism may seek water, food, warmth, shade, or salt in response to physiological deviation.

Behavior is therefore one mechanism by which organisms regulate internal state.

== Adaptive behavior ==

A behavior can be adaptive if it increases fitness under relevant conditions.

Adaptive value is empirical and context-dependent.

A behavior that was historically adaptive can become maladaptive in a changed environment.

Observed behavior should not automatically be assumed to be optimal.

== Human behavior ==

Human behavior is shaped by biological, psychological, cultural, institutional, and social processes.

Research methods include:

* observation;
* experiments;
* surveys;
* digital traces;
* physiological measurements;
* longitudinal studies.

Human actions also involve norms, language, explicit goals, and social institutions.

Biological explanation should not erase these levels.

== Computational behavior ==

Software agents and robots can be assigned operational behaviors such as moving, choosing actions, communicating, or updating state.

This use is legitimate when behavior is explicitly defined at the system level.

Behavioral similarity between software and organisms does not establish biological identity, subjective experience, or consciousness.

== Failure modes in behavioral inference ==

Common errors include:

* anthropomorphism;
* assuming function from appearance;
* confusing correlation with mechanism;
* treating one trial as a stable trait;
* ignoring internal state;
* ignoring observation bias;
* pseudo-replication;
* treating inactivity as absence of behavior;
* inferring cognition from one behavioral pattern.

Strong claims require converging evidence.

